# Component styling

Use component props for component behavior and variants. Use classes or `style` for instance layout, and override semantic tokens when a subtree needs a different theme.

<DocsTable columns={targetColumns} rows={targetRows} minWidth="var(--ds-sizing-md)" />

## Svelte components

Public components forward `class`, `style`, and relevant native attributes to their layout root. Classes from a global application stylesheet or a utility framework can be used directly:

```svelte
<AesirButton class="toolbar-action">Deploy</AesirButton>
```

```css
/* app.css */
.toolbar-action {
  margin-inline-start: auto;
}
```

No `!important` is required for ordinary layout adjustments. Component-scoped Svelte styles still stop at a component boundary. If the rule must remain in the consuming component's local `<style>` block, mark that selector as global:

```svelte
<AesirButton class="toolbar-action">Deploy</AesirButton>

<style>
  :global(.toolbar-action) {
    margin-inline-start: auto;
  }
</style>
```

For a single adjustment, an inline style is the shortest option:

```svelte
<AesirButton style="margin-inline-start: auto">
  Deploy
</AesirButton>
```

Use a wrapper when it represents real application layout, such as a toolbar, stack, or grid-not only to reach a component's rendered element.

### Floating content

Dropdown, Popover, and Tooltip render their floating panel separately from the trigger. `class` and `style` target the trigger root; `contentClass` and `contentStyle` target the panel:

```svelte
<AesirDropdown
  class="toolbar-action"
  contentClass="project-actions"
  contentStyle="width: var(--ds-sizing-3xs)"
  {items}
>
  {#snippet trigger(props)}
    <AesirButton variant="outline" {...props}>Actions</AesirButton>
  {/snippet}
</AesirDropdown>
```

Define `project-actions` in a global stylesheet or use `:global(.project-actions)` in a component-scoped style block.

## Standalone HTML

HTML components are Custom Elements. A class or inline style targets the element host and works with ordinary CSS:

```html
<aesir-button class="toolbar-action">Deploy</aesir-button>
```

```css
.toolbar-action {
  margin-inline-start: auto;
}
```

When a Custom Element is written directly in a Svelte template, a local scoped selector also works on its host because the element is part of that template:

```svelte
<aesir-button class="toolbar-action">Deploy</aesir-button>

<style>
  .toolbar-action {
    margin-inline-start: auto;
  }
</style>
```

### Shadow DOM boundary

The component's rendered controls live inside Shadow DOM. A descendant selector from the application cannot reach them:

```css
/* Does not cross Shadow DOM */
.toolbar-action button {
  border-radius: 0;
}
```

Use public attributes for component options:

```html
<aesir-button
  variant="destructive"
  width="full"
  radius="none"
>
  Delete project
</aesir-button>
```

CSS custom properties inherit through Shadow DOM, so semantic tokens can theme a component or an entire subtree:

```css
.settings-panel {
  --ds-accent: var(--ds-emphasis-purple);
  --ds-accent-hover: var(--ds-emphasis-hover-purple);
  --ds-accent-ring: var(--ds-border-active-purple);
}
```

```html
<section class="settings-panel">
  <aesir-button variant="accent">Save settings</aesir-button>
</section>
```

Prefer semantic variables such as `--ds-accent`, `--ds-surface`, and `--ds-foreground` over primitive color values. This preserves light and dark theme behavior.
